{"concepts_used":["GC-0078","GC-0058","GC-0120","GC-0093"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~2","quote":"To be capable of distinguishing the applicant's goods, the scent needs to be something apart from the goods themselves. It should be neither a natural characteristic nor an expected characteristic of the product, but something added to identify the applicant's goods from those of others in the same market."},{"ref":"TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1","quote":"The natural scent of a product will have no inherent adaptation to distinguish the goods."},{"ref":"TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1","quote":"A masking scent has a functional purpose, and is not capable of distinguishing for that reason."},{"ref":"TMA1995/s33","quote":"(1) The Registrar must, after the examination, accept the application unless he or she is satisfied that: (a) the application has not been made in accordance with this Act; or (b) there are grounds under this Act for rejecting it."}],"answer":"Yes—as a statement of the Manual’s practice on a scent’s capacity to distinguish, rather than an absolute statutory rule about acceptance.

**Manual practice:** The scent should be separate from the goods themselves: neither a natural nor an expected characteristic, but something added to identify the applicant’s goods from those of others in the same market (TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~2). The Manual states that a product’s natural scent has no inherent adaptation to distinguish (TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1). Merely adding a scent is not enough: a functional masking scent is also described as not capable of distinguishing (TMM/Part21/7/7/2/1~1).

**Act:** Section 33(1) requires acceptance unless the Registrar is satisfied that the application does not comply with the Act or that there are statutory grounds for rejection (TMA1995/s33). The supplied legislation does not set out a scent-specific rule or the statutory provisions governing capacity to distinguish. These passages therefore do not establish that every natural-scent mark must ultimately be refused, or that every added scent can be accepted."}
